exercises.reference:
import re
from collections import defaultdict

def run_mapreduce(inputs, map_fn, reduce_fn):
    groups = defaultdict(list)
    for key, value in inputs:
        for k, v in map_fn(key, value):
            groups[k].append(v)
    out = {}
    for k in sorted(groups):
        for k2, v2 in reduce_fn(k, groups[k]):
            out[k2] = v2
    return out

def map_fn(doc_id, text):
    for word in set(re.findall(r"[a-z]+", text.lower())):
        yield word, doc_id

def reduce_fn(word, doc_ids):
    yield word, sorted(set(doc_ids))

exercises.solution:
The key of the intermediate pairs is what we want to group by — the word — and the value is what we want to collect — the document id:

```python
def map_fn(doc_id, text):
    for word in set(re.findall(r"[a-z]+", text.lower())):
        yield word, doc_id

def reduce_fn(word, doc_ids):
    yield word, sorted(set(doc_ids))
```

Taking `set(...)` in the mapper means "rain" appearing three times in document 2 produces one pair, not three — the same saving a combiner gives. The reducer still deduplicates, which keeps it correct even if a mapper task is re-executed after a failure and its output is read twice. Google's original MapReduce paper lists inverted-index construction among the jobs it ran.

